part propeller "Propeller"
category Actuators
summary "A spinning propeller: thrust and drag torque grow with the square of speed, thrust fades as the aircraft climbs."
explain "Propeller theory in coefficients: thrust T = ρ·n²·D⁴·C_T and torque Q = ρ·n²·D⁵·C_Q, with n in rev/s. Moving along the thrust axis at v raises the advance ratio J = v/(n·D) and lowers thrust, here linearly: C_T(J) = C_T0·(1 − J/J₀), zero at J₀. The motor must supply Q; the airframe receives T."
tradeoffs "Bigger diameter: more thrust per watt (efficient hover) but slower response and more inertia; higher pitch: more speed, more torque. Match the motor's Kv: large props want low-Kv motors."
limits "Axial flight only, no ground effect, blade flapping or stall; linear C_T(J); air density constant."
pairs part.bldc_motor rotational.inertia translational.mass translational.load_force
port shaft rotational
port airframe translational
param diameter m ~ 0.254 "Diameter (10 in = 0.254 m)"
param thrust_coefficient 1 = 0.11 "C_T at zero advance"
param torque_coefficient 1 = 0.006 "C_Q"
param zero_thrust_advance 1 = 0.8 "Advance ratio J₀ at which thrust vanishes"
param density kg/m^3 = 1.225 "Air density"
param reference_speed rad/s = 1000 "Speed the derived thrust and torque are quoted at"
let n = shaft.w/6.283185307
let thrust = density*diameter^4*thrust_coefficient*(n*abs(n) - abs(n)*airframe.v/(zero_thrust_advance*diameter))
flow shaft = density*diameter^5*torque_coefficient*n*abs(n)
flow airframe = -thrust
derive "static thrust at the reference speed" = density*diameter^4*thrust_coefficient*(reference_speed/6.283185307)^2 N
derive "torque at the reference speed" = density*diameter^5*torque_coefficient*(reference_speed/6.283185307)^2 N·m
derive "hover power at the reference speed" = density*diameter^5*torque_coefficient*(reference_speed/6.283185307)^2*reference_speed W
